@* Tab order is the family's: the Reminders timeline comes first because it is what the kiosk opens
   on and what an idle one returns to, so the leftmost tab and the landing view agree. *@
<div class="view-tabs mb-4">
    @* Each tab's words sit in an element of their own rather than loose inside the button, because
       the Reminders button holds more than its label: the badge below is inside it too, so the
       button's own text is the label PLUS a bell and a number whenever something falls today. The
       tab strip is read back as a list of labels (the family's tab ORDER is a requirement), and
       that reading has to mean "the labels" rather than "whatever each button happens to contain" —
       otherwise the order assertion starts failing on a day when something is due. No CSS of its
       own: .view-tab is already a flex row, so the span is simply its first flex item. *@
    <button data-testid="reminders-tab"
            class="view-tab @(CurrentView == DashboardView.Reminders ? "active" : "")"
            @onclick="() => OnViewChanged.InvokeAsync(DashboardView.Reminders)">
        <span class="view-tab__label">Reminders</span>
        @* The list below has no "Today" panel to glance at any more — each row names its own day
           instead — so the tab strip is what tells the family something falls today, from
           whichever tab they are on. Nothing is rendered at all when the count is zero: an empty
           pill or a "0" left on screen would be a standing claim where silence is the signal. *@
        @if (TodayReminderCount > 0)
        {
            @* The bell is decoration — the span's aria-label carries the whole message — so it is
               hidden rather than announced as "bell". *@
            <span class="view-tab__badge" data-testid="reminders-tab-badge" aria-label="@TodayBadgeLabel">
                <span aria-hidden="true">🔔</span>
                @TodayReminderCount
            </span>
        }
    </button>
    <button data-testid="month-tab"
            class="view-tab @(CurrentView == DashboardView.Month ? "active" : "")"
            @onclick="() => OnViewChanged.InvokeAsync(DashboardView.Month)"><span class="view-tab__label">Month View</span></button>
    <button data-testid="agenda-tab"
            class="view-tab @(CurrentView == DashboardView.MonthAgenda ? "active" : "")"
            @onclick="() => OnViewChanged.InvokeAsync(DashboardView.MonthAgenda)"><span class="view-tab__label">Agenda</span></button>
    <button data-testid="day-tab"
            class="view-tab @(CurrentView == DashboardView.Day ? "active" : "")"
            @onclick="() => OnViewChanged.InvokeAsync(DashboardView.Day)"><span class="view-tab__label">Day View</span></button>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public DashboardView CurrentView { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<DashboardView> OnViewChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How many events fall on the kiosk's local today. Above zero the Reminders tab carries a bell
    /// and this number; at zero it carries neither, so the badge never has to say "nothing".
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int TodayReminderCount { get; set; }

    // The badge in words. A bell glyph and a bare digit tell a screen reader nothing, and a wall
    // display is not the only way this page is read.
    private string TodayBadgeLabel =>
        TodayReminderCount == 1 ? "1 reminder today" : $"{TodayReminderCount} reminders today";
}
